<mujoco model="xarm6_pusht_single_obstacle_real">
  <!-- Real-table push past one pudding box between block start and goal.

       The obstacle is placed to reproduce ../xarm6_pusht_tabletop/
       single_obstacle.xml's LAYOUT, not its coordinates: the two scenes push
       different objects over different distances, so copying the cube's
       absolute offset would not give the same task. What is copied is the
       obstacle's position in the start->goal frame, as fractions of the
       journey.

       Sim: start (0, 0) -> goal (0.2, 0.75), |d| = 0.7762 m, cube at
       (0.2, 0.5). Resolving that into the path frame gives
         along  = 0.6888 of |d|
         lateral = -0.0830 of |d|   (right of the direction of travel)
       Real: start (0.381, 0.343) -> goal (0.381, -0.305), |d| = 0.648 m,
       direction (0, -1). The same two fractions, carried over by the
       rotation that maps sim's travel direction onto this one:
         0.381 + (-0.0830 * 0.648) = 0.327
         0.343 + (-0.6888 * 0.648) = -0.103
       Was (0.30, 0.02), which the file itself called a placeholder.

       That still obstructs, which is the point of the scene: rotated 90 deg
       the box reaches to x = 0.3715, and the block's crossbar reaches back to
       x = 0.3365, so they overlap by 3.5 cm across the straight-line route.
       Keep in sync with SCENES["single_obstacle_real"] in oim/utils/scenes.py;
       tests/test_scenes.py checks the two agree. -->
  <include file="common_real.xml"/>
  <include file="tee_real.xml"/>
  <worldbody>
    <camera name="front" pos="1.4907 0.3750 0.8684" xyaxes="0.0000 1.0000 -0.0000 -0.4226 0.0000 0.9063"/>
    <geom class="obstacle" name="obs_1" type="box" size="0.054 0.0445 0.0298" pos="0.327 -0.103 0.03" euler="0 0 90"/>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>